const MORE = {
ci:[
 {d:3,p:'Biologist Rachel Carson\'s 1962 book <i>Silent Spring</i> is often credited with launching the modern environmental movement. Yet Carson framed the book less as a call to protest than as a demand for honest information: she argued that the public had a right to know the risks of chemical pesticides before those chemicals were sprayed on their towns and farms.',
  q:Q_MAIN, c:['Although <i>Silent Spring</i> is linked to environmental activism, Carson presented it mainly as an argument for the public\'s right to information about pesticides.','Carson opposed every form of environmental activism.','<i>Silent Spring</i> was the first book ever written about pesticides.','Pesticides were sprayed mainly on farms rather than in towns.'],
  e:'"Yet" turns the text toward its real point: Carson framed the book as a right-to-know argument. The other choices overstate or invent claims.'},
 {d:2,p:'From the train window, the fields Daniyar had known all his life seemed to shrink and blur, as if someone were folding up the landscape and putting it away. He pressed his forehead to the glass and did not look at the city ticket in his hand until the last familiar poplar had vanished.',
  q:Q_MAIN, c:['Daniyar is reluctant to leave behind the place he has always known.','Daniyar is excited to travel for the first time.','Daniyar is worried that he has lost his ticket.','The fields near Daniyar\'s home are being destroyed.'],
  e:'He watches the familiar fields until the very end and avoids looking at the city ticket, which shows reluctance to leave.'},
 {d:3,p:'Researchers removed the foragers from several honeybee colonies. Soon afterward, some young "nurse" bees, which normally stay inside the hive, began foraging days or even weeks earlier than usual. The researchers concluded that the division of labor in a colony is not fixed by age but can shift in response to the colony\'s needs.',
  q:Q_MAIN, c:['Honeybee colonies can change which bees perform which tasks when the colony\'s needs change.','Nurse bees are better at foraging than older bees.','Removing foragers usually causes a colony to collapse.','Young bees never leave the hive.'],
  e:'The final sentence states the conclusion the experiment supports: jobs shift with the colony\'s needs.'},
 {d:2,p:'The Great Wall of China is often said to be visible from space with the naked eye, but astronauts have reported that it is very hard to see from low orbit. The wall is long but narrow, and its color closely matches the surrounding land.',
  q:'According to the text, why is the Great Wall hard to see from orbit?', c:['It is narrow and blends in with the land around it.','It is usually hidden by clouds.','It is shorter than most people believe.','Astronauts rarely travel in low orbit.'],
  e:'The text gives two reasons: the wall is narrow, and its color matches the land.'},
 {d:3,p:'Economist Elinor Ostrom studied communities that share resources such as fishing grounds and forests. Many economists had assumed that shared resources are always overused unless a government or private owner controls them. Ostrom documented many cases in which local users created their own rules, monitored one another, and kept the resources healthy for generations.',
  q:Q_MAIN, c:['Ostrom found evidence that communities can manage shared resources well without outside control.','Ostrom argued that governments should own all forests.','Shared resources are always overused.','Fishing grounds are harder to manage than forests.'],
  e:'Ostrom\'s cases challenge the assumption in the second sentence. "Always overused" is the view she disproved.'},
],
ev:[
 {d:3,p:'A historian claims that early photographs were often staged to tell a story rather than simply recording what was in front of the camera.',
  q:'Which quotation from the historian\'s essay most effectively supports the claim?', c:['"Photographers routinely moved props, and even people, into position before taking a picture, arranging scenes for dramatic effect."','"Early cameras required exposures lasting several seconds."','"Photographs quickly became popular in newspapers."','"Many viewers believed photographs showed exactly what the eye could see."'],
  e:'Only the first quotation shows photographers arranging scenes. The last one describes what viewers believed, not what photographers did.'},
 {d:2,p:'Ecologists hypothesize that a lizard species has shorter legs on islands where most perches are thin branches, because shorter legs grip thin branches more easily.',
  q:'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the hypothesis?', c:['Lizards on islands with mostly thin branches had shorter legs than lizards of the same species on islands with mostly thick trunks.','Short-legged lizards ran faster on open ground.','The islands have fewer predators than the mainland.','Lizards on all the islands ate similar insects.'],
  e:'Support must connect branch thickness to leg length within the same species.'},
 {d:3,p:'A researcher claims that a new reading app raised students\' test scores, because students who used the app scored higher than students who did not.',
  q:'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the claim?', c:['The students who used the app were also in smaller classes with more experienced teachers.','Students said they enjoyed using the app.','The app was used most often in the evening.','Some students used the app more than others.'],
  e:'If app users also had smaller classes and stronger teachers, those differences could explain the higher scores.'},
 {d:3,p:'A student claims that in a memoir, the narrator suggests that her grandmother\'s strictness hid deep affection.',
  q:'Which quotation from the memoir most effectively illustrates the claim?', c:['"She scolded me for every crooked stitch, yet each night she quietly unpicked my mistakes and redid them so my quilt would be perfect by morning."','"She rarely smiled and never praised my sewing."','"Her house always smelled of wool and black tea."','"She taught sewing at the village school for forty years."'],
  e:'The claim has two sides: strictness (scolding) and hidden affection (secretly fixing the quilt). Only the first quotation shows both.'},
 {d:3,p:'Scientists claim that a soil fungus helps trees share nutrients and that younger trees benefit most from this sharing.',
  q:'Which finding, if true, would most directly support both parts of the claim?', c:['When the fungus linked trees, nutrients moved from older trees to nearby seedlings, and linked seedlings grew faster than unlinked ones.','The fungus grows in many different kinds of forests.','Older trees linked by the fungus grew taller than unlinked older trees.','Nutrients moved between trees only in summer.'],
  e:'Both parts are needed: nutrients are shared, and seedlings gain the most. The older-tree finding points the wrong way.'},
],
qe:[
 {d:2,p:tbl('Adults who get news from each source (%)',['Source','2018','2024'],[['Print newspapers','38','21'],['Television','64','55'],['Social media','45','61'],['News websites','52','58']])+'<p>A student claims that between 2018 and 2024, social media had the largest increase of any news source.</p>',
  q:'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the claim?', c:['Social media use rose 16 percentage points, while news websites rose only 6 and the other sources declined.','Television remained the most-used source in 2024.','Print newspaper use fell by 17 percentage points.','58% of adults got news from websites in 2024.'],
  e:'"Largest increase" needs a comparison of changes. The television choice is false: social media (61%) passed television (55%) in 2024.'},
 {d:3,p:tbl('Wheat yield (tons per hectare)',['Plot','Dry year','Wet year'],[['No fertilizer','2.1','3.0'],['Fertilizer','2.3','4.2']])+'<p>Researchers claim that the fertilizer was much more effective in the wet year than in the dry year.</p>',
  q:'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the claim?', c:['In the wet year the fertilizer added 1.2 tons per hectare, but in the dry year it added only 0.2.','Fertilized plots produced 4.2 tons per hectare in the wet year.','All plots produced more in the wet year than in the dry year.','Plots without fertilizer produced 2.1 tons in the dry year.'],
  e:'"Effectiveness" means the gain from fertilizer: 4.2 − 3.0 = 1.2 versus 2.3 − 2.1 = 0.2. Higher yields overall are true but beside the point.'},
 {d:2,p:bars('Average homework per night, by grade','Minutes',[['6',30],['7',40],['8',55],['9',70],['10',85],['11',95],['12',80]]),
  q:'Which choice most accurately describes data from the graph?', c:['Homework time rose with each grade from 6 through 11 and then fell in grade 12.','Homework time rose with every grade from 6 through 12.','Grade 12 students had the most homework.','Grade 8 students had twice as much homework as grade 6 students.'],
  e:'The bars climb to 95 in grade 11, then drop to 80. Grade 8 (55) is less than double grade 6 (30).'},
 {d:3,p:'<p>A city tested bike-sharing in two neighborhoods. Neighborhood A, population 40,000, recorded 12,000 rides. Neighborhood B, population 15,000, recorded 9,000 rides. An official claims that residents of Neighborhood B used the program more heavily.</p>',
  q:'Which choice best supports the official\'s claim?', c:['Neighborhood B had 0.6 rides per resident, compared with 0.3 in Neighborhood A.','Neighborhood A recorded more total rides.','Neighborhood A has the larger population.','Both neighborhoods recorded at least 9,000 rides.'],
  e:'"More heavily" is a rate: 9,000 ÷ 15,000 = 0.6 and 12,000 ÷ 40,000 = 0.3. Total rides is the trap.'},
 {d:2,p:tbl('Temperature and ice cream sales at a beach kiosk',['Day','Temperature (°C)','Sales (units)'],[['Mon','22','140'],['Tue','25','180'],['Wed','19','90'],['Thu','28','240'],['Fri','30','260']])+'<p>The owner concludes that sales tend to rise as the temperature rises.</p>',
  q:'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?', c:['The coolest day, Wednesday (19°C), had the fewest sales (90), and the hottest day, Friday (30°C), had the most (260).','Tuesday\'s sales were 180 units.','Thursday was warmer than Monday.','The kiosk sold more than 900 units over the five days.'],
  e:'A trend needs both variables moving together. The correct choice links the lowest temperature with the lowest sales and the highest with the highest.'},
],
inf:[
 {d:3,p:'Medieval maps of the world often placed Jerusalem at the center and filled distant regions with imaginary creatures. Historians argue that these maps were not mainly tools for travel. Instead, the maps most likely ___',
  q:'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c:['expressed their makers\' beliefs about the meaning and order of the world.','helped merchants plan the fastest trade routes.','were drawn by people who had traveled widely.','were seen only by sailors.'],
  e:'If they were not travel tools and showed imaginary creatures, they served another purpose: expressing a worldview.'},
 {d:2,p:'Two groups of students studied the same list of words. One group was told a test would follow; the other was told nothing. Both groups were then tested, and their scores were nearly identical. This result suggests that ___',
  q:'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c:['knowing about the test did not change how well students remembered the words in this case.','students always learn better when they expect a test.','the word list was too easy for both groups.','the students who were told nothing did not study.'],
  e:'Identical scores mean the warning made no difference here. "Always" overreaches.'},
 {d:3,p:'The workers who built the pyramids at Giza were once thought to have been enslaved. However, archaeologists have found the workers\' tombs near the pyramids, along with evidence that they received regular food rations and medical care for injuries. These findings suggest that ___',
  q:'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c:['the workers may have been laborers who were valued and supported by the state.','the pyramids were built in only a few years.','all of the workers came from foreign lands.','medical care did not exist in ancient Egypt.'],
  e:'Honorable burial, food, and medical care point to valued workers, not enslaved people. The last choice contradicts the evidence.'},
 {d:2,p:'Some songbirds learn their songs from adults during a short period early in life. Researchers raised young birds of one species in soundproof rooms where they heard no adult songs. As adults, these birds sang simple, unusual songs. This suggests that ___',
  q:'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c:['hearing adults sing is important for young birds to develop their species\' typical song.','songbirds cannot sing at all without training.','soundproof rooms damage birds\' hearing.','adult birds prefer simple songs.'],
  e:'The birds still sang, so "cannot sing at all" is too strong. Missing adult songs led to unusual songs.'},
 {d:3,p:'An author released a new novel as an e-book and as a print book at the same price. In cities, the e-book sold more copies; in rural areas, the print edition sold more. Rural areas in the region also have fewer stores selling e-readers and slower internet service. One explanation for the pattern is that ___',
  q:'Which choice most logically completes the text?', c:['rural readers had less convenient access to the technology needed for e-books.','rural readers prefer longer novels.','the e-book cost more in rural areas.','city readers dislike printed books.'],
  e:'The text pairs the sales pattern with limited e-reader stores and slow internet. The price was the same everywhere.'},
],
wic:[
 {d:3,p:'The committee\'s report was notably ___: rather than blaming any single agency, it described the failures of each with equal care and restraint.',
  q:Q_WORD, c:['evenhanded','partisan','sensational','perfunctory'],
  e:'Treating every agency "with equal care and restraint" is fair and balanced: evenhanded. Perfunctory means done with little care.'},
 {d:2,p:'Strict fishing limits helped <u>check</u> the decline of the cod population, which finally began to recover after a decade.',
  q:'As used in the text, what does the word "check" most nearly mean?', c:['restrain','examine','mark','pay'],
  e:'Limits that let a population recover held back its decline, so "check" means restrain.'},
 {d:3,p:'Although the scientist\'s early papers were ___, filled with jargon that even experts struggled to follow, her later books were praised for their clarity.',
  q:Q_WORD, c:['opaque','lucid','concise','popular'],
  e:'"Although" contrasts the early papers with the later clarity, and "struggled to follow" confirms they were hard to understand: opaque.'},
 {d:2,p:'Investors hope the land will <u>appreciate</u> in value once the new railway station opens nearby.',
  q:'As used in the text, what does the word "appreciate" most nearly mean?', c:['increase','admire','understand','thank'],
  e:'Land that gains value after a station opens increases in value.'},
 {d:2,p:'Rather than simply ___ the old theory, the young physicist showed that it was a special case of a broader principle, preserving what the theory got right.',
  q:Q_WORD, c:['discarding','memorizing','celebrating','teaching'],
  e:'"Rather than" sets up the opposite of "preserving": throwing the theory away, or discarding it.'},
],
ts:[
 {d:2,p:'Many people picture inventors working alone in sudden flashes of insight. <u>The light bulb, however, was the product of decades of work by dozens of researchers in several countries, each improving on earlier designs.</u> Thomas Edison\'s team tested thousands of materials before settling on a practical filament.',
  q:'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', c:['It offers an example that challenges a popular image of how inventions happen.','It explains why Edison\'s filament succeeded.','It summarizes the text\'s final conclusion.','It presents a view that the rest of the text supports.'],
  e:'"However" signals that the light bulb story contradicts the lone-genius picture from the first sentence.'},
 {d:2,p:'The kettle sang, the cat stretched on the windowsill, and the morning light spread slowly across the table, warming the bread and the old wooden chairs. For the first time in weeks, Shoira let herself sit down.',
  q:'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', c:['To convey a peaceful moment that allows a character to finally rest','To explain how to prepare breakfast','To suggest that Shoira dislikes her home','To describe a busy household preparing for guests'],
  e:'Gentle details build calm, and "for the first time in weeks" shows Shoira finally resting.'},
 {d:2,p:'Some scientists once argued that dinosaurs were slow, cold-blooded reptiles. Fossil evidence of feathers, fast growth, and bird-like breathing has led most researchers to reject that view. Today, many paleontologists consider birds to be living dinosaurs.',
  q:'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', c:['It describes an earlier view, presents evidence against it, and states the current view.','It compares two current theories without choosing between them.','It lists the steps scientists use to find fossils.','It argues that birds and dinosaurs are unrelated.'],
  e:'Old view, then evidence against it, then today\'s view.'},
 {d:3,p:'A city council expected its new bus lane to shorten commutes. <u>Commute times did fall, but by less than one minute on average.</u> Council members are now debating whether the lane is worth its cost.',
  q:'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?', c:['It reports a result that was positive but smaller than expected.','It shows that the bus lane failed completely.','It explains why the council built the lane.','It provides evidence that commutes became longer.'],
  e:'Times did fall (positive), but barely, which explains the debate that follows.'},
 {d:3,p:'Critics praise the composer\'s symphonies, but her film scores are rarely discussed. That is a mistake: the scores contain some of her boldest experiments with rhythm, techniques she later used in the very symphonies critics admire.',
  q:'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', c:['To argue that an overlooked part of the composer\'s work deserves attention','To explain why film music is less important than symphonies','To describe how symphonies are written','To criticize the composer\'s symphonies'],
  e:'"That is a mistake" signals an argument: the neglected film scores matter.'},
],
ct:[
 {d:3,p:'<p><b>Text 1</b><br>Some researchers argue that social media use directly causes anxiety in teenagers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b><br>Large studies find only a weak link between total screen time and anxiety. The effects seem to depend on how teens use social media, such as whether they constantly compare themselves with others, rather than on how much time they spend.</p>',
  q:'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the researchers in Text 1?', c:['By suggesting that their claim is too simple, since the type of use matters more than the amount','By agreeing that social media causes anxiety in all teenagers','By arguing that teenagers should spend more time online','By claiming that no studies have examined the issue'],
  e:'Text 2 does not deny any effect. It says the picture is more complicated than direct cause and effect.'},
 {d:2,p:'<p><b>Text 1</b><br>The poet\'s later work abandons the clear imagery of her early poems for abstract language, and it is weaker as a result.</p><p><b>Text 2</b><br>Her later poems are more abstract, but this shift reflects her growing interest in philosophy, and the poems reward readers willing to engage with their ideas.</p>',
  q:'Which choice best describes how the author of Text 2 relates to Text 1?', c:['The author agrees that the later poems are more abstract but disagrees that they are weaker.','The author agrees that the later poems are weaker.','The author denies that the later poems are more abstract.','The author argues that the early poems are more philosophical.'],
  e:'"Her later poems are more abstract, but..." concedes the fact and rejects the judgment.'},
 {d:3,p:'<p><b>Text 1</b><br>Planting trees is the most effective way for cities to cool down during heat waves.</p><p><b>Text 2</b><br>Trees help, but they take decades to grow. In the short term, reflective roofs and pavements can lower surface temperatures much more quickly.</p>',
  q:'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?', c:['By noting a limitation of the strategy Text 1 favors and proposing a faster alternative','By rejecting the idea that trees cool cities','By agreeing that trees are the fastest solution','By arguing that heat waves are not a serious problem'],
  e:'"Trees help, but..." accepts the benefit, points out the time problem, and offers another option.'},
 {d:3,p:'<p><b>Text 1</b><br>Historians have long believed that the ancient city was abandoned suddenly after an earthquake.</p><p><b>Text 2</b><br>New excavations show that residents removed their valuables and sealed doorways over several years before leaving. Earthquake damage appears mostly in buildings that were already empty.</p>',
  q:'Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?', c:['Text 2 provides evidence that challenges Text 1\'s account of how the city was abandoned.','Text 2 confirms that the city was abandoned suddenly.','Text 2 argues that the earthquake never happened.','Text 2 explains why historians study earthquakes.'],
  e:'A gradual departure before the quake contradicts a sudden abandonment. Text 2 does not deny the earthquake itself.'},
 {d:3,p:'<p><b>Text 1</b><br>Learning a second language in childhood is essential, because adults rarely become fluent.</p><p><b>Text 2</b><br>Children often have an advantage in pronunciation, but studies show that motivated adults frequently learn grammar and vocabulary faster than children do.</p>',
  q:'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?', c:['By arguing that Text 1 overstates children\'s advantage, since adults can learn some parts of a language faster','By agreeing that adults can never become fluent','By claiming that pronunciation is the only important skill','By saying children learn grammar faster than adults'],
  e:'Text 2 grants children one advantage (pronunciation) but shows adults ahead in others.'},
],
tr:[
 {d:2,p:'The bridge\'s original design called for steel cables. ___ engineers chose cables made of a new carbon-fiber material, which was lighter and did not rust.',
  q:Q_TRANS, c:['Ultimately,','Similarly,','For example,','In addition,'],
  e:'The final choice replaced the original plan, so "Ultimately" fits.'},
 {d:3,p:'Many early critics dismissed photography as a mechanical process rather than an art. ___ some photographers began reworking their images by hand to make them look more like paintings.',
  q:Q_TRANS, c:['In response,','Likewise,','In summary,','For instance,'],
  e:'The photographers reacted to the critics, so "In response" shows the relationship.'},
 {d:2,p:'Octopuses have no bones. ___ they can squeeze through any opening larger than their beak, the only hard part of their body.',
  q:Q_TRANS, c:['As a result,','However,','Similarly,','In contrast,'],
  e:'Having no bones is the cause; squeezing through tiny openings is the effect.'},
 {d:2,p:'Some economists predicted that the new tax would sharply reduce consumer spending. ___ spending barely changed in the months after the tax took effect.',
  q:Q_TRANS, c:['Instead,','Therefore,','Furthermore,','For instance,'],
  e:'What happened was the opposite of the prediction, so "Instead" fits.'},
 {d:2,p:'The novel\'s first half follows a mother\'s life in a small mountain village. ___ the second half follows her daughter in a crowded city decades later.',
  q:Q_TRANS, c:['By contrast,','Therefore,','For example,','Thus,'],
  e:'Two halves with opposite settings: a contrast.'},
],
rs:[
 {d:2,p:notes(['Al-Khwarizmi was a 9th-century scholar who worked in Baghdad.','He came from the region of Khwarazm, in present-day Uzbekistan.','His book on solving equations gave its name to "algebra."','The word "algorithm" comes from a Latin form of his name.']),
  q:'The student wants to emphasize al-Khwarizmi\'s influence on the vocabulary of mathematics. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c:['Both "algebra" and "algorithm" trace back to al-Khwarizmi: one comes from his book on equations, the other from his name.','Al-Khwarizmi came from the region of Khwarazm, in present-day Uzbekistan.','Al-Khwarizmi was a 9th-century scholar who worked in Baghdad.','Al-Khwarizmi wrote a book on solving equations.'],
  e:'The goal is vocabulary, and only one choice names the two words he gave to mathematics.'},
 {d:2,p:notes(['Tardigrades are tiny animals about half a millimeter long.','They can survive extreme cold, heat, and radiation.','In 2007, tardigrades survived ten days exposed to outer space.','They survive by drying out and almost completely stopping their metabolism.']),
  q:'The student wants to explain how tardigrades survive extreme conditions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c:['Tardigrades survive extreme conditions by drying out and almost completely stopping their metabolism.','In 2007, tardigrades survived ten days in outer space.','Tardigrades are about half a millimeter long.','Tardigrades can survive extreme cold, heat, and radiation.'],
  e:'"How" asks for the method. The space trip shows that they survive, not how.'},
 {d:3,p:notes(['The Amazon carries more water than any other river.','The Nile is often listed as the world\'s longest river, at about 6,650 km.','The Amazon is usually measured at about 6,400 km.','Some recent measurements suggest the Amazon is actually longer than the Nile.']),
  q:'The student wants to highlight a disagreement about the rivers\' lengths. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c:['Although the Nile is often listed as the longest river, some recent measurements suggest the Amazon is longer.','The Amazon carries more water than any other river.','The Nile is about 6,650 km long.','The Amazon is usually measured at about 6,400 km.'],
  e:'A disagreement needs two competing claims, joined here by "Although."'},
 {d:2,p:notes(['In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed mold killing bacteria in a lab dish.','The mold produced a substance he named penicillin.','Mass production of penicillin began in the 1940s.','Penicillin became one of the first widely used antibiotics.']),
  q:'The student wants to emphasize how long it took for the discovery to be widely used. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c:['Although Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928, it was not mass-produced until the 1940s.','Fleming noticed mold killing bacteria in a lab dish.','Penicillin became one of the first widely used antibiotics.','The mold produced a substance Fleming named penicillin.'],
  e:'Emphasizing a delay requires both dates.'},
 {d:2,p:notes(['The Registan is a public square in Samarkand.','It is framed by three madrasas built between the 15th and 17th centuries.','The oldest, the Ulugh Beg Madrasa, was completed in 1420.','It is part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.']),
  q:'The student wants to describe the Registan to an audience unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', c:['The Registan, a public square in Samarkand, is framed by three madrasas built between the 15th and 17th centuries.','The Ulugh Beg Madrasa was completed in 1420.','The oldest madrasa on the square is named after Ulugh Beg.','The Registan is part of a UNESCO World Heritage site.'],
  e:'A newcomer first needs to know what and where the Registan is.'},
],
bd:[
 {d:2,p:'The Afrosiyob high-speed train connects Tashkent and Samarkand in just over two ___ the same trip once took most of a day.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['hours;','hours,','hours','hours, and,'],
  e:'Both halves are complete sentences, so a semicolon is needed. A comma alone creates a splice.'},
 {d:3,p:'The museum\'s newest exhibit ___ rare manuscripts from Bukhara, Samarkand, and Khiva—opens next week.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['—a collection of','a collection of',', a collection of','; a collection of'],
  e:'The phrase closes with a dash, so it must open with a dash. Pairs must match.'},
 {t:'tap', d:3, s:'The novel, which was published in *1925* is still widely read today.',
  e:'The extra clause opened with a comma after "novel," so it must close with one: "published in 1925, is still..."'},
 {d:2,p:'Some scientists study ___ others study the chemistry of distant planets.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['volcanoes on Earth;','volcanoes on Earth,','volcanoes on Earth','volcanoes on Earth: and'],
  e:'Two complete sentences: a semicolon joins them correctly.'},
 {d:2,p:'The Bukhara walking tour includes the city\'s three most famous ___ the Kalyan Minaret, the Ark fortress, and the Lyabi-Hauz pond.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['sights:','sights, such as:','sights;','sights, which are:'],
  e:'A complete sentence introduces a list, so a colon is right. Never put a colon after "such as" or "are."'},
],
fss:[
 {d:3,p:'The results of the experiment, which was conducted at two universities, ___ remarkably similar.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['were','was','is','has been'],
  e:'The subject is "results" (plural). The clause about the experiment is extra information.'},
 {t:'tap', d:2, s:'Each of the paintings in the gallery\'s new collection *were* donated by local artists.',
  e:'"Each" is singular, so the verb must be "was."'},
 {d:2,p:'Built in the 12th century, ___',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['the Kalyan Minaret has survived wars and earthquakes.','wars and earthquakes have not destroyed the Kalyan Minaret.','travelers still admire the Kalyan Minaret.','the city\'s people have protected the Kalyan Minaret.'],
  e:'The minaret was built, so it must come right after the comma.'},
 {d:3,p:'By the time the rescue team arrived, the hikers ___ for nearly two days.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['had been waiting','have been waiting','are waiting','will wait'],
  e:'An action that continued up to another past event takes the past perfect: "had been waiting."'},
 {d:2,p:'The museum returned the ancient vase to Greece, whose government had requested ___ return for years.',
  q:Q_CONV, c:['its','their','it\'s','they\'re'],
  e:'The return belongs to the vase (singular), so use the possessive "its." "It\'s" means "it is."'},
],
};
UNITS.forEach(u=>{
  u.lessons.forEach((L,li)=>L.forEach((it,k)=>{ if(!it.d) it.d = li===0 ? (k<2?1:2) : (k<3?2:3); }));
  if(MORE[u.id]) u.lessons.push(MORE[u.id]);
});

